Publish an internal service so it is reachable at
https://<name>.<your local domain>.

The certificate and the name's answer are already arranged — one
wildcard certificate covers every published name, and the dns module
already serves the suffix. So this takes a name and a target, and
nothing else.

Examples:
  olr ingress add grafana --device nuc --port 3000
  olr ingress add nas --device synology --port 5001 --scheme https
  olr ingress add hello --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
  olr ingress add mi --host 172.16.1.163 --port 80 --header 'Host: 172.16.1.163'

Usage:
  olr ingress add <name> [flags]

Flags:
      --device olr devices list     device to publish, by its name in olr devices list
      --header stringArray          set an upstream request header as 'Name: value', repeatable
  -h, --help                        help for add
      --host string                 address or hostname to publish, for a target that is not a device
      --link-path string            page path to open and use for its icon (e.g. /ui); does not change proxy routing
      --port uint16                 port the service listens on
      --remove-header stringArray   remove an upstream request header, repeatable
      --scheme string               how to reach it: http, https (default http)

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
